{"description":"M3b — a standard declares the checks that verify it, which is what turns a documented standard into an enforced one. The pattern was just proven for the exchange corpus in the 1.4 work, where agreement between the corpus, the declaration and the shipped bytes became a gate rather than a claim; this item widens that pattern to the standard family. Only mechanical standards qualify: a standard that declares checks becomes executable, and one that cannot stays honestly a review checklist. Depends on M3a, which is the executor.","acceptanceCriteria":"- A standard artifact may declare checks: each with a command and a scope, validated by the same malformed-declaration rules as M1's binding field.\n- eka verify runs declared checks for the current repository and resolves each check to the constraint id that declared it, so a violation names its standard.\n- A standard with no checks is accepted and reported as checklist-only, never silently treated as enforced.\n- At least one standard in this repository declares a real check, and removing the check makes the gate pass a change it previously caught (the non-vacuity proof)."}